   344|        <h2>Why Pay for Coordination? Here's the Honest Answer.</h2>
   345|        
   346|        <div class="why-item">
   347|            <div class="num">1</div>
   348|            <div>
   349|                <h4>You could book the hospital yourself—but would you pick the right doctor?</h4>
   350|                <p>We match based on your specific case, procedure volume, and outcomes data. The hospital's international department will book you with whoever is available—not necessarily the best person for your condition.</p>
   351|            </div>
   352|        </div>
   353|        
   354|        <div class="why-item">
   355|            <div class="num">2</div>
   356|            <div>
   357|                <h4>You could translate records yourself—but would they be medically accurate?</h4>
   358|                <p>We use coordinators familiar with Chinese medical terminology. Generic translation services often miss clinically significant details that affect how doctors approach your case.</p>
   359|            </div>
   360|        </div>
   361|        
   362|        <div class="why-item">
   363|            <div class="num">3</div>
   364|            <div>
   365|                <h4>You could navigate the system alone—but what if something goes wrong?</h4>
   366|                <p>We stay connected after you leave China. When our clients have post-treatment questions or concerns, they have someone who knows their case and can reach their medical team. That's the value most people don't think about until they need it.</p>
   367|            </div>
   368|        </div>
   369|        
   370|        <p style="margin-top: 40px; font-size: 1.1rem; color: #444; max-width: 650px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;">We're transparent about what we do and what we don't do. Our coordinators are real people—not bots. And we take responsibility for the coordination work we commit to.</p>
